<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Syrian Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Great Druze Revolt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Syrian Revolt was a rising against French Mandate rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> from 1925 to 1927. It began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-al-atrash">Sultan al-Atrash</a> and spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and other regions, where nationalists joined it. French forces bombarded Damascus in October 1925 and eventually crushed the revolt, but it shaped Syrian nationalism and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to allow a Syrian constituent assembly.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-syrian-revolt.jpg" alt="Great Syrian Revolt" width="320" height="602" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alatrash Khaldoun · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Great_Syrian_Revolution.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1918 Ottoman forces withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> after defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allied Powers</a> and their Hashemite Arab allies. The British had promised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-hashim">Hashemites</a> a united Arab state, even as the Allies made other plans in the 1916 Sykes–Picot Agreement. In March 1920 the Hashemites established the Kingdom of Syria under Faisal, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> as capital, but it was short-lived. Under the French Mandate, administrators were placed at every level of government with the stated aim of training Syrians, but in practice they did the work themselves, which local notables resented. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> also restricted the tribes, banning arms in settled areas and imposing lump taxes on livestock.</p><p>The spark was the treatment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a>. In 1923 the leaders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a> reached an agreement with the French, hoping for the autonomy they had enjoyed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. After Selim al-Atrash resigned, the majlis chose a French officer, Captain Cabrillet, as governor, and his term was extended indefinitely.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> revolted in September 1925 the French had only 14,397 men and officers in the Mandate, with 5,902 Syrian auxiliaries, down from 70,000 in 1920. The Druze won a series of victories, annihilated a French relief column and captured the fort at al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwayda">Suwayda</a>. The French withdrew for the winter, which the new high commissioner, Henry de Jouvenel, called a tactical error, since it encouraged a regional rebellion to take on national dimensions.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-al-atrash">Sultan al-Atrash</a> led the revolt from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, Hasan al-Kharrat led an assault in which he briefly captured the residence of the high commissioner, Maurice Sarrail, before withdrawing under heavy bombardment. On 4 October 1925 Fawzi al-Qawuqji led an uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a>, and Mohammed al-Ayyash planned a front along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> at Deir ez-Zor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The revolt was defeated and the Syrians failed in their main goal of independence and the removal of French forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> was bombed by air for 24 continuous hours, and some villages in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a> were emptied after being destroyed and burned. The death toll reached 4,213, of whom 2,064 died in Jabal al-Druze. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> nevertheless reunified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, which it had divided into four states, removed its high commissioner and some officers, and held elections won by the national opposition led by Ibrahim Hananu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hashim-al-atassi">Hashim al-Atassi</a>. The revolt increased Syrian nationalism and unity after earlier sectarian divisions. A memorial edifice in al-Qurayya, begun in 1987, opened in 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Syrian Revolt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Syrian Revolt</b> of 1925-1927, led by Sultan al-Atrash, was crushed after French artillery shelled Damascus.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-mandate-for-syria-and-the-lebanon">French Mandate for Syria and the Lebanon</a></li><li><blockquote>Druze anger led Sultan al-Atrash to rise in July 1925, defeating French columns and sparking the <b>Great Syrian Revolt</b>, which spread to Damascus before it was crushed in 1927.</blockquote><a href="/state/jabal-druze-state">Jabal Druze State</a></li><li><blockquote>Months after its creation, the <b>Great Syrian Revolt</b> spread from the Jabal Druze to Damascus and the countryside; French forces shelled Damascus in October 1925 and suppressed the rising by 1927.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-syria">State of Syria</a></li></ul></section>
